SECTION C.  THE RISK L52 NAMED -- where can the auxiliary's Hessian degenerate?
======================================================================================================================

  C.0  The elimination, done here rather than quoted.  Write the longitudinal magnitude as w = |W| and
  v = |V|, and f(w) = J(w.w) so that f''(w) = 2 J_Y + 4 J_YY w^2 = 2 sigma_L.  The repair's scalar
  potential is F(v, w) = f(w) + Lambda (v - w)^2.

  [PASS] C1  the auxiliary's own Hessian is f''(w) + 2 Lambda = 2(sigma_L + Lambda) -- derived, not quoted
  [PASS] C1b the elimination w(v) exists and is single-valued iff dv/dw = 1 + f''/(2 Lambda) never vanishes, i.e. iff sigma_L > -Lambda -- a ONE-SIDED condition, so an INFINITE sigma_L is harmless and only a sufficiently negative one is fatal   (dv/dw = 1 + f''(w)/(2 Lambda); the map is monotone, hence globally invertible, whenever this is > 0)
  [PASS] C1c and with sigma_L = 1/Delta'(s) (the deposited theory's own identity, sec.4.1) and kappa = 1/Lambda, the degeneracy locus is EXACTLY Delta'(s) = -kappa, which is the same thing as Delta_eff' = 0.  The repair therefore cannot hide its own failure: wherever the auxiliary stops being eliminable, the repaired stiffness is simultaneously infinite.   (sigma_L + Lambda = 0  <=>  1/Delta' = -1/kappa  <=>  Delta' = -kappa  <=>  Delta' + kappa = 0)

